Scrape availability|up{job="api"}|A PromQL selector returns the latest scrape-success signal for matching targets.|Check whether the collector can currently retrieve target metrics.|1 means a successful scrape and 0 a failed scrape; missing targets may produce no series.|{instance="host:9090",job="api"} 1|PromQL query, not shell. Scrape health is not application health.
Counter rate|rate(http_requests_total{job="api"}[5m])|Calculates a reset-aware per-second rate for each counter series.|Turn a cumulative request counter into recent throughput.|Each result retains source labels; apply rate before aggregating different instances.|{instance="a",status="200"} 12.5|Illustrative metric; use a window with enough samples for the scrape interval.
Aggregated throughput|sum by (job) (rate(http_requests_total[5m]))|Aggregates per-series request rates by service job.|Compare service traffic without averaging raw counters.|One output series per job shows requests per second across instances.|{job="api"} 125|PromQL; label names and counting semantics must match instrumentation.
Error ratio|sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="api",status=~"5.."}[5m])) / sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="api"}[5m]))|Computes a ratio of server-error requests to total requests.|Monitor a defined failure population alongside traffic.|0.02 means 2% under this definition; absent traffic requires separate interpretation.|0.02|PromQL example; 5xx-only may omit business failures or client-visible timeouts.
Classic histogram p95|histogram_quantile(0.95, sum by (le) (rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{job="api"}[5m])))|Estimates p95 latency from cumulative classic histogram buckets.|Inspect the slow tail across instances.|Result is seconds; le must remain in aggregation and bucket layout limits precision.|0.25|PromQL for classic histograms; native histogram syntax and semantics differ.
Mean latency|sum(rate(http_request_duration_seconds_sum{job="api"}[5m])) / sum(rate(http_request_duration_seconds_count{job="api"}[5m]))|Divides observed duration accumulation by observation count.|Compare mean latency with tail behavior.|The unit is seconds per observation; a healthy mean can coexist with a bad p99.|0.08|PromQL; requires matching sum/count series and a nonempty population.
Absent target signal|absent(up{job="api"})|Returns a signal when no matching series exists.|Detect disappearance from discovery rather than only failed scrapes.|A returned 1 means the selector has no series; an empty result means at least one match exists.|{job="api"} 1|PromQL; use expected-target inventory for per-instance absence rather than this service-wide check alone.
Counter resets|resets(http_requests_total{job="api"}[1h])|Counts observed decreases in a counter over one hour.|Correlate rate changes with restarts or instrumentation resets.|A positive result identifies resets within that series; replacement labels can instead create a new series.|{instance="a"} 2|PromQL; resets are not automatically application failures.
Configuration validation|promtool check config prometheus.yml|Validates Prometheus configuration and referenced rule files.|Catch invalid configuration before a controlled reload.|SUCCESS indicates accepted syntax and referenced-file checks, not target reachability.|SUCCESS: prometheus.yml is valid prometheus config file syntax|Local validation; use a trusted configuration and installed promtool matching deployment expectations.
Rule validation|promtool check rules alerts.yml|Checks recording and alerting rule syntax.|Find invalid expressions before publishing rules.|Read file and rule errors; a valid expression can still measure the wrong population.|SUCCESS: ... rules found|Local read-only validation; add rule tests for behavioral expectations.
Rule unit tests|promtool test rules tests.yml|Runs rule tests against supplied input series and expected results.|Verify alert timing and query behavior under resets, gaps and failures.|PASS means the specified cases matched; untested scenarios remain unknown.|SUCCESS|Runs local test definitions; fixtures should include missing data and recovery.
Metrics endpoint sample|curl -fsS --max-time 5 http://127.0.0.1:9090/metrics|Retrieves a local Prometheus-format metrics endpoint.|Check that exposition exists and inspect metric names and labels.|HELP and TYPE describe metric families; sample labels define individual series.|# TYPE prometheus_build_info gauge|Read-only HTTP request; endpoint and port are examples and output may expose infrastructure metadata.
Metric series count|count(http_requests_total)|Counts currently selected time series for a metric.|Check whether instrumentation changes increase active series unexpectedly.|The result is series count, not requests. Stale or absent series are excluded from the current instant vector.|240|PromQL in a compatible backend; the metric name is illustrative. Compare with normal label dimensions and ingestion statistics.
Cardinality by service|count by (job) (http_requests_total)|Counts selected series grouped by job.|Locate which service contributes to a cardinality increase.|Each output sample is one job with its active series count; it is not traffic volume.|{job="api"} 180|PromQL; adapt the grouping label to your instrumentation and avoid unbounded investigative queries.
Recent counter increase|increase(http_requests_total{job="api"}[1h])|Estimates counter increase over one hour with reset handling.|Estimate request volume over a bounded investigation window.|Values can be fractional because of extrapolation; each input series produces its own increase.|{job="api",status="200"} 12500|PromQL; sufficient samples and scrape coverage are required. This is not a billing-grade exact event ledger.
Peak queue depth|max_over_time(queue_depth{queue="orders"}[30m])|Returns the largest sampled gauge value in the window.|Find a sampled backlog peak that is hidden by the current value.|The maximum is over collected samples, not every moment between scrapes.|{queue="orders"} 450|PromQL; queue_depth is an example gauge. Missing short spikes require better instrumentation or sampling.
Queue trend|deriv(queue_depth{queue="orders"}[10m])|Estimates the per-second slope of a gauge using regression.|Check whether a backlog is growing or draining over a window.|Positive values mean increasing sampled depth; negative values mean decreasing depth. This is not a counter rate.|{queue="orders"} 2.5|PromQL for float gauge samples; choose a window long enough to reduce short-term noise.
Scrape sample volume|scrape_samples_post_metric_relabeling{job="api"}|Reads the number of samples retained from each scrape after metric relabeling.|Investigate a sudden instrumentation payload increase.|Each value is a sample count per target scrape, not the cumulative number stored over time.|{job="api",instance="api-1:8080"} 350|PromQL over Prometheus-generated scrape metrics; adapt target labels.
Scrape latency|scrape_duration_seconds{job="api"}|Reads the duration of recent target scrapes.|Find targets approaching the configured scrape timeout.|Values are seconds per target; compare with its timeout and scrape interval.|{job="api",instance="api-1:8080"} 0.42|PromQL; a slow scrape may reflect exporter cost, network delay or target pressure.
Sample freshness|time() - timestamp(up{job="api"})|Computes the age in seconds of the selected up sample.|Inspect freshness of existing scrape-health samples.|A larger age indicates an older selected sample; missing/stale series may disappear rather than yield a large age.|{job="api",instance="api-1:8080"} 12|PromQL; combine with absent-series checks and configured lookback/staleness behavior.
Latency threshold fraction|sum(rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{job="api",le="0.3"}[5m])) / sum(rate(http_request_duration_seconds_count{job="api"}[5m]))|Calculates the fraction of sampled requests at or below 300 ms using a classic histogram.|Evaluate a latency threshold directly when a matching bucket exists.|A result of 0.97 means 97% of requests counted in this population met the threshold; zero traffic needs explicit handling.|0.97|PromQL; requires the exact 0.3 bucket, consistent units and matching populations. Missing buckets are not zero compliance.
Largest error contributors|topk(5, sum by (service) (rate(http_requests_total{status=~"5.."}[5m])))|Ranks services by recent server-error rate.|Identify the largest error-volume contributors during triage.|Values are errors per second, not error percentages; high-traffic services can dominate the ranking.|{service="checkout"} 8.2|PromQL; service and status labels are illustrative. Inspect ratios and user impact as well.
Prometheus instant API query|curl -fsS --get --data-urlencode 'query=up' http://127.0.0.1:9090/api/v1/query|Queries the Prometheus HTTP API for an instant-vector expression.|Inspect structured query results when building a diagnostic script.|Check status, resultType and result; sample values are encoded as timestamp/value pairs. HTTP success alone is not application health.|{"status":"success","data":{"resultType":"vector","result":[...]}}|Read-only HTTP request to a local trusted endpoint; configure authentication safely if required.
Prometheus rule API|curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:9090/api/v1/rules|Retrieves loaded recording and alerting rule status.|Investigate rule evaluation errors or differences from a configuration file.|Groups contain rules; health and lastError describe evaluation, while alert state is a separate property.|"health":"ok","lastError":""|Read-only query; protect internal alert labels and use the intended Prometheus instance.
